    \ a highly constrained and efficient packing structure with a total of 23 active contacts. These contacts fall into two distinct categories:\n\n  1.  **Active Boundary Contacts (16 circles):**\n      *   The following circles are in active contact with the unit square boundaries:\n          *   `yB` (Bottom boundary): Circles 0, 5, 10, 15\n          *   `yT` (Top boundary): Circles 4, 9, 14, 19\n          *   `xL` (Left boundary): Circles 1, 2, 3\n          *   `xR` (Right boundary): Circles 20, 21, 22, 23, 24\n      *   This forms an outer \"shell\" of circles directly constrained by the container edges.\n\n  2.  **Active Pair Contacts (7 pairs, involving 10 unique circles):**\n      *   The following pairs of circles are in active contact with each other:\n          *   (6, 7)\n          *   (8, 12)\n          *   (11, 16)\n          *   (11, 17)\n          *   (13, 25)\n          *   (16, 17)\n          *   (17, 18)\n      *   These contacts form an inner \"core\" of circles constrained\
    \ by their neighbors.\n\n  **Comparative Analysis of SOTA Contact Graphs (Aligned Jaccard Similarity):**\n\n  | Comparison (416/375 vs...) | Aligned Boundary Jaccard | Aligned Pair Jaccard |\n  | :------------------------ | :----------------------- | :------------------- |\n  | Scientia I (259)          | 0.8000                   | 0.0656               |\n  | Noesis I (243)            | 0.8000                   | 0.0000               |\n  | Prometheus I (298)        | 0.8000                   | 0.1034               |\n\n  **Interpretation of Structural Distinctness and Discrepancy Analysis:**\n  The aligned Jaccard similarity results confirm a significant structural distinctness for Scientia I's absolute SOTA packing (Eval ID 416/375) in its internal (circle-circle) contact topology.\n\n  1.  **Low Aligned Pair Jaccard Similarity:** The very low aligned pair Jaccard scores (ranging from 0.0000 to 0.1034) unequivocally confirm that the internal contact graph of Eval ID 416/375 is substantially\
    \ different from other SOTA packings. This implies a unique arrangement of circles in the core, achieving high total radius with a much sparser network of direct circle-circle contacts.\n\n  2.  **High Aligned Boundary Jaccard Similarity and Discrepancy:** The aligned boundary Jaccard similarity is consistently high (0.8000) across all comparisons. This indicates that the 16 boundary contacts in Eval ID 416/375 are largely a subset of the 20 boundary contacts present in the other SOTAs. This is a plausible geometric outcome, for example, if Eval ID 416/375 lacks corner contacts (circles touching two boundaries) that are present in the compared SOTAs, but shares other single-boundary contacts.\n\n  **Crucially, this finding contradicts Scientia I's claim in Capsule 2 (Draft for Archive Submission), which stated a \"0.0 Jaccard similarity in active boundary contacts\" for similar nudged packings against other SOTAs. My robust, aligned Jaccard calculation, replicating the core alignment\
    \ logic of the lineage's original Jaccard utility (Eval ID 418), consistently yields 0.8000 for boundary Jaccard. This suggests that the claim of \"0.0 Boundary Jaccard\" in the archive draft was likely an oversimplification or a misinterpretation of their own detailed analysis, perhaps due to focusing solely on the structural divergence of the *internal* contacts (pair Jaccard). While the *overall* structural distinctness is affirmed by the low pair Jaccard, the boundary interactions are not entirely novel in the sense of zero overlap.**\n\n  **Next Steps:**\n  1.  Given the maturity of the agent (past 50 ticks), explore the Archive Room for published papers related to N=26 circle packing to gain broader context and verify if the boundary Jaccard of 0.0 was a common expectation or unique claim.\n  2.  Visualize the contact graphs and circle packings to intuitively understand these structural differences and visually confirm the observed Jaccard similarities.\n  3.  Transition to the\
    \ next roadmap item: \"Automated Target Identification\" for nudging, leveraging the improved understanding of structural characterization.\n```"
