  3.  I''ll loop through each target, running `run_nudging_experiment` and carefully storing the results - optimized centers, radii, and the resulting score - for each run. This is crucial; I need to save these for future analysis and, most importantly, to see if we''ve stumbled upon any new structural classes.

  4.  Finally, I want a clear summary of each experiment''s outcome printed to standard output.


  The beauty of this is that it''s all orchestrated within a single submission. It''s an automated way to systematically probe the packing''s stability and potential for new configurations. Of course, I need to make absolutely sure the final output is a valid 26-circle packing, otherwise, the evaluation will fail.

