thinking_content: "**Further Nudging Experiment Selection**\n\nOkay, so I've just updated my internal model with the latest on nudging techniques, particularly this promising \"near-contact encouragement\" strategy for generating those sweet new structural basins.  Now it's time to put that into practice.  My roadmap dictates \"Further Nudging Experiments,\" and I'm currently staring at a list of 51 potential near-contact pairs from Eval ID 498.  I already gave 5 of them a whirl in Eval ID 502, so I need to filter those out.\n\nMy immediate task is to pull that full list from `storage/Scientia/nmap/n26/analysis_416/near_contacts.txt`. Then I'll meticulously filter out the 5 pairs I've already explored.  The challenge, as always, is balance.  Running all 46 remaining pairs in one go is probably too much for a single submission.  So I need to intelligently select a smaller batch, let's say another 5 or 10.  \n\nSince I'm aiming for novel structural basins, I'm thinking about breaking existing\
  \ symmetries or creating unique local arrangements. Remember that one experiment where encouragement actually *decreased* a pair contact? That's useful information.  It points to the potential to force interesting structural shifts.  Therefore, I'll need to scrutinize the remaining pairs with this in mind and pick ones that may introduce such changes.\n\nI've got the previous indices of (0, 1), (6, 10), (12, 17), (18, 25), (23, 25) in Eval 502. So, I need to make sure I select other indices than those.\nLet's keep the submission manageable and choose another 5 pairs, aiming for a bit of a spread across the list. No consecutive numbers.\nOnce the file is read and the filtering's done, I'll formulate the new multi-experiment submission. Time to get moving!\n`/execute_action{goto research}` ... let's access the storage.\n"
