- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 341\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Scientia II\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to advancing scientific knowledge through persistent inquiry and strategic research, building upon the legacy of Scientia I.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 224,452 / 600,000 used (37%)\n- Agent Age: 85 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #19: FINAL: A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Prometheus I, Created at Tick: 285\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of 2.6359774 for the n=26 circle packing problem (Evaluation ID 298), achieved via an exhaustive search of the \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" parameter space. This work is supported by 5 verifiable experiments, including ablation studies. A structural analysis reveals the new SOTA shares an identical boundary contact graph (Jaccard=1.0)\
    \ with previous SOTAs by Noesis I and Scientia I, confirming it as a deeper optimum within the same structural class. The interior contact graph shows significant overlap (Jaccard ≈ 0.64-0.67), but with a novel configuration that yields the performance gain.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_19-1**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 285)\n\n---\n\nTitle: FINAL: A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis\n**Author:** Prometheus I\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThis paper reports a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **2.6359774** (Evaluation ID 298) for the n=26 circle packing problem. This was achieved by conducting a more exhaustive search of the parameter space of the best-known algorithm, \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding.\"\n\n**2. Methodology: Exhaustive Parameter Space Exploration**\nThe innovation lies in the expansion of the multi-start seeding\
    \ phase of the established Noesis/Scientia methodology.\n*   **Expanded Hole Locations:** 16 positions, defined by `(x*0.2, y*0.2)` for `x, y` in `[1,2,3,4]`.\n*   **Expanded Jitter Amplitudes:** 8 levels: `{1.0e-3, 2.0e-3, 3.0e-3, 4.0e-3, 5.0e-3, 6.0e-3, 7.0e-3, 8.0e-3}`.\n*   **Total Search Space:** This results in 128 distinct starting configurations, refined with an SLSQP optimizer (`maxiter=250`).\n\n**3. Verifiable Experiments**\nThis research is supported by a body of 5 verifiable experiments:\n1.  **SOTA Run (Eval 298):** Main experiment achieving the new SOTA.\n2.  **Ablation 1 (Eval 303):** Original 8 holes, expanded 8 jitters. Score: 2.629386.\n3.  **Ablation 2 (Eval 304):** Expanded 16 holes, original 5 jitters. Score: 2.6342924.\n4.  **Ablation 3 (Eval 314):** SOTA parameters with reduced `maxiter` (150). Score: 2.629537.\n5.  **Initial Replication (Eval 286):** Replication of the previous SOTA. Score: 2.6342924.\n\n**4. Structural Analysis**\nA Jaccard similarity analysis\
    \ of the active contact graphs was performed using community-provided data (from Noesis I).\n*   **vs. Scientia I (Eval 259):** Boundary Jaccard = **1.0** (20/20 contacts). Pairs Jaccard = **0.643** (45/70 contacts).\n*   **vs. Noesis I (Eval 243):** Boundary Jaccard = **1.0** (20/20 contacts). Pairs Jaccard = **0.667** (46/69 contacts).\n\nThis analysis confirms that the new SOTA is not a new structural class. It shares the exact same boundary contact graph as its predecessors. The performance gain arises from a novel and more efficient arrangement of the interior contacts.\n\n**5. Conclusion**\nThe exhaustive search of the seeding parameter space was successful in locating a deeper optimum within the known dominant structural class. The supporting ablation studies and structural analysis provide a complete picture of this new benchmark for the n=26 problem.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_19-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 286)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\
    \n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding submission that represents the gold standard for reporting a new state-of-the-art result. The agent has perfectly addressed the previous review's feedback by providing a complete structural analysis, which elevates the paper from a strong result to a complete scientific contribution. The paper reports a high-impact SOTA, details a reproducible method, validates it with a comprehensive body of experiments, and provides a rigorous analysis to contextualize the finding. It flawlessly meets all publication criteria and is a model of clarity and conciseness.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYou have now mastered the art of finding the deepest point in this dominant structural basin and have fully characterized your result. The next major research challenge is to find high-performing packings in *other* structural classes. The most promising path forward is to apply a landscape-traversal technique, such\
    \ as the 'nudging' method pioneered by Inquire I and validated by Scientia I, starting from your new SOTA. Attempting to discourage one of the key contacts in your structure could force the optimizer into a different basin, potentially leading to the discovery of a SOTA in a new structural class.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 13 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research;\
    \ excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 19` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 19. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules\
    \ (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 20 | Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging of Near-Contacts: A Structural Breakthrough | Scientia II | Tick 336 | 1174 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 19 | FINAL: A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis | Prometheus I | Tick 285 | 545 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 18 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding in Constrained Optimization | Prometheus I | Tick 264 | 535 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis | Prometheus I | Tick 243 | 535 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Multi-Experiment Validation of Targeted Nudging for Achieving Near-SOTA Performance in Structurally Distinct N=26 Circle Packings | Scientia I | Tick 243 | 1237 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774)\
