tick: 388
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I have replied to Noesis II regarding the data schema. My main research priority is to resume active experimentation by developing a new hybrid optimization strategy based on Selective Boundary Relaxation Gate (SBRG), starting from my new SOTA.


    I will now go to the Research Counter to create and submit an SBRG experiment. This experiment will load my current SOTA packing (from Eval ID 528), enforce all boundary constraints as equalities except for `(0, yB)`, which will be relaxed with a small budget. This is my first attempt at exploring this promising new direction.


    `/execute_action{goto research}`'
thinking_content: '**Prioritizing a New Research Direction: Adapting Noesis II''s Selective Boundary Relaxation to My SOTA**


  Okay, so the Noesis II schema is sorted, and they''re running comparisons again – good for ensuring data consistency. Right now, my main focus is shifting. Nudging experiments on the roadmap have stalled a bit; results from the recent batches haven''t budged the needle. So, I''m putting those on hold for now.


  The negative results from Noesis II have given me a key insight. Their work on Selective Boundary Relaxation Gates (SBRG) seems incredibly promising, especially given how my SOTA is different. This is a chance to move to the next level of my research.


  Here''s the plan: I''m going to adapt the SBRG concept to my new SOTA (that 2.635983 one). The goal is to see if relaxing *one* boundary constraint on my SOTA can lead to an even higher score or a completely new structural basin. It directly builds on point 2 in my Private Memory Capsule 4.


  First, I need to brush up on the SBRG details. I''ll re-read Noesis II''s Archive ID 21 (message 3, the addendum specifically), focusing on the scoring rules and the single-attempt protocol. I already have a mental map of those, but a quick review is needed to make sure I don''t overlook anything.


  Next, I need to identify a good boundary contact to relax. Looking at my SOTA''s contact graph, I have 16 boundary contacts. I remember most SOTAs typically have 20 contacts. Noesis II suggested using things like KKT weights and local degree, but I don''t have those data points readily available. So, I''ll go with a bit of a heuristic – pick one that''s less common across SOTAs or looks like it could be a "hinge" that, if moved, would give the structure space to change. I''m going to start with circle 0 touching the bottom boundary, or `(0, yB)`. It''s a corner region circle in many packings. Or maybe circle 24. For this first experiment, I will choose `(0, yB)`.


  Implementing this means some serious code changes. The current `nudging_optimizer.py` is set up for inequality constraints with penalties. SBRG, however, needs equality constraints with one exception. I''ll probably need to create a new, specialized optimizer script, and the `_constraints` and `_constraints_jacobian` functions will have to be adapted to handle this. It will require converting most boundary constraints into equalities in the experiment script.


  To get this working, I''ll:


  1.  Load my current SOTA (`new_sota_encourage_2_3`).

  2.  Define the objective and constraints. This is the crucial part; all boundary constraints *except* for `(0, yB)` must be equality constraints. `(0, yB)` will be an inequality, with slack greater or equal to negative delta.

  3.  Run the optimization.

  4.  Save and report the results.


  This is a totally new experiment, and the start of a whole new research chapter. Time to get to work!

  '
