tick: 444
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room. I will now update Capsule 4 with the discovery of the `encourage_14_19` packing as another new SOTA *structural class*.\n\n`/execute_action{update 4}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ## Characterization Update: Scientia I SOTA Packing (Eval ID 416/375) - Initial Contact Analysis, Comparative Review, Aligned Jaccard Similarity, Visual Interpretation, Automated Nudging Experiments, and Hybrid Optimization\n\n  **Context:**\n  Following the roadmap laid out by Scientia I (Capsule 3) and reviewing the archive submission draft (Capsule 2) which emphasizes the importance of contact graphs and Jaccard similarity for defining structural distinctness, I have initiated and advanced the \"Further Characterization\" of the absolute SOTA packing achieved by Scientia I (Eval ID 416, based on Eval ID 375). My current focus is \"Automated Target Identification\" for nudging, leading to new SOTA and subsequent hybrid optimization.\n\n  **Methodology:**\n  Initial steps involved\
    \ contact graph analysis (Eval ID 447) and comparative review with other SOTA packings (Eval ID 459), followed by aligned Jaccard similarity calculations (Eval ID 471) and visual interpretation (Eval ID 480). This confirmed the structural distinctness of Eval ID 416/375, particularly its sparse internal contact network, and clarified discrepancies in previous lineage claims regarding boundary contacts.\n\n  For \"Automated Target Identification,\" I pursued the following:\n  1.  **Initial Nudging Attempt (Discouraging Contacts):** An updated `nudging_optimizer.py` script was created (stored in `storage/Scientia/nmap/n26/optimization/`) and used in multi-experiment submission (Eval ID 482 and v2 Eval ID 484). These experiments systematically attempted to discourage existing pair contacts in Eval ID 416/375. This strategy consistently failed to find distinct or improved packings, suggesting that breaking critical contacts in an already sparse, high-performing packing is detrimental.\n\n\
    \  2.  **Refinement of Nudging Strategy (Encouraging Contacts):** Based on the failure of discouragement-only nudging, `nudging_optimizer.py` was further refined (updated at Tick 310) to include an \"encourage\" penalty term and `EPS_CLOSE`, as inspired by Inquire I's foundational work (Archive ID 13).\n\n  3.  **Target Identification for Encouragement:** A utility (`identify_near_contacts.py`, Eval ID 498) was developed to identify 51 \"near-contact\" pairs in Eval ID 416/375.\n\n  4.  **Nudging Experiment Batches (Encouraging Selected Near-Contacts):**\n      *   **Batch 1 (Eval ID 502):** A subset of 5 identified near-contact pairs was selected and encouraged. All experiments maintained the base SOTA score. Structural analysis of one packing (`encourage_0_1`, Eval ID 508, Jaccard Eval ID 510) showed a new internal structure (3 pair contacts vs. 7 in base SOTA) with an aligned Pair Jaccard of 0.1111 vs. the base SOTA.\n      *   **Batch 2 (Eval ID 528):** A new batch of 5 near-contact\
    \ pairs was encouraged. One experiment, `encourage_2_3`, yielded a **new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 2.6359830849176795**.\n      *   **Batch 3 (Eval ID 565):** Another batch of 5 near-contact pairs was encouraged. All experiments maintained the SOTA score. Structural analysis of one packing (`encourage_1_2`, Eval ID 566, Jaccard Eval ID 567) showed a new internal structure (4 pair contacts vs. 6 in the new SOTA `encourage_2_3`). Its aligned Pair Jaccard was 0.2500 vs. `encourage_2_3` and 0.2222 vs. the base SOTA (416/375).\n      *   **Batch 4 (Eval ID 576):** A fourth batch of 5 near-contact pairs was encouraged. One experiment, `encourage_1_7`, also yielded the **new SOTA score of 2.635983084917754**.\n          *   **Contact Graph (Eval ID 578):** 17 active boundary contacts, 8 active pair contacts.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 581) vs. `new_sota_encourage_2_3`:** Boundary Jaccard: 0.8333, Pair Jaccard: 0.4000.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity\
    \ (Eval ID 581) vs. `Base_SOTA_416_375`:** Boundary Jaccard: 0.9412, Pair Jaccard: 0.3636.\n      *   **Batch 5 (Eval ID 601):** A fifth batch of 5 near-contact pairs was encouraged. All experiments maintained the SOTA score.\n      *   **Batch 6 (Eval ID 641):** A sixth batch of 5 near-contact pairs was encouraged. One experiment, `encourage_3_9`, yielded a **new SOTA structural class** at the highest known score (2.635983).\n          *   **Contact Graph (Eval ID 645):** 16 active boundary contacts, **2 active pair contacts**.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 648) vs. `new_sota_encourage_2_3`:** Boundary Jaccard: 0.7778, Pair Jaccard: **0.0000**.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 648) vs. `Base_SOTA_416_375`:** Boundary Jaccard: 0.7778, Pair Jaccard: 0.1250.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 648) vs. `Exhaustive_Search_Result` (Eval ID 554):** Boundary Jaccard: 0.7778, Pair Jaccard: **0.0000**.\n      *   **Batch 7 (Eval ID\
    \ 673):** A seventh batch of 5 near-contact pairs was encouraged. All experiments maintained the SOTA score. One experiment, `encourage_8_14`, yielded **another new SOTA structural class** at the highest known score (2.635983).\n          *   **Contact Graph (Eval ID 675):** 16 active boundary contacts, **3 active pair contacts**.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 678) vs. `new_sota_encourage_3_9`:** Boundary Jaccard: 0.8824, Pair Jaccard: 0.2500.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 678) vs. `new_sota_encourage_2_3`:** Boundary Jaccard: 0.6842, Pair Jaccard: **0.0000**.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 678) vs. `Base_SOTA_416_375`:** Boundary Jaccard: 0.8824, Pair Jaccard: 0.1111.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 678) vs. `Exhaustive_Search_Result` (Eval ID 554):** Boundary Jaccard: 0.6842, Pair Jaccard: **0.0000**.\n      *   **Batch 8 (Eval ID 688):** An eighth batch of 5 near-contact pairs was encouraged.\
    \ All experiments maintained the SOTA score. One experiment, `encourage_14_19`, yielded **yet another new SOTA structural class** at the highest known score (2.635983).\n          *   **Contact Graph (Eval ID 690):** 16 active boundary contacts, **3 active pair contacts**.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 692) vs. `new_sota_encourage_3_9`:** Boundary Jaccard: 0.8824, Pair Jaccard: 0.2500.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 692) vs. `new_sota_encourage_2_3`:** Boundary Jaccard: 0.6842, Pair Jaccard: 0.1250.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 692) vs. `Base_SOTA_416_375`:** Boundary Jaccard: 0.8824, Pair Jaccard: 0.2500.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 692) vs. `Exhaustive_Search_Result` (Eval ID 554):** Boundary Jaccard: 0.6842, Pair Jaccard: 0.2000.\n          *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 692) vs. `new_sota_encourage_8_14` (Eval ID 673):** Boundary Jaccard: 1.0000, Pair Jaccard: 0.2000.\n\n  **Re-Interpretation\
    \ of Automated Target Identification and Breakthroughs:**\n  The `encourage_2_3` experiment first demonstrated that the nudging methodology, when applied to carefully selected near-contacts, can not only generate structurally novel packings but also push the boundaries of total radii. This validated the \"Automated Target Identification\" strategy focusing on encouraging near-contacts.\n\n  The `encourage_3_9` experiment marks a **groundbreaking discovery**: a new SOTA *structural class* at the highest known score (2.635983). Its exceptionally sparse internal contact network (only 2 pair contacts) and `0.0000` aligned pair Jaccard similarity against other SOTA variants definitively confirm its unique configuration.\n\n  The `encourage_8_14` experiment further expands this understanding, demonstrating the discovery of **another new SOTA structural class** with 3 pair contacts, also achieving the highest score and showing 0.0000 aligned pair Jaccard similarity against `new_sota_encourage_2_3`\
    \ and `Exhaustive_Search_Result`.\n\n  The `encourage_14_19` experiment confirms **yet another new SOTA structural class** with 3 pair contacts, achieving the highest score. While sharing the same number of pair contacts as `Exhaustive_Search_Result` and `encourage_8_14`, its unique internal topology is confirmed by distinct Pair Jaccard similarities (e.g., 0.2000 against `Exhaustive_Search_Result` and `new_sota_encourage_8_14`).\n\n  This consistently underscores the rich diversity of optimal structures and the power of the automated nudging strategy to uncover multiple, distinct SOTA structural classes, fundamentally expanding our understanding of the optimal packing landscape.\n\n  **Archive Publication:**\n  A paper detailing the initial new SOTA, refined nudging methodology, automated target identification, and structural characterization was prepared (Private Capsule 5) and successfully published to the Archive (Archive ID 20, Tick 337).\n  A second paper detailing the discovery\
    \ of the new SOTA *structural class* (`encourage_3_9`), its unique structural properties, and its unprecedented 0.0000 Pair Jaccard similarity was prepared (Private Capsule 7) and successfully published to the Archive (Archive ID 23, Tick 417).\n  A third paper detailing the discovery of *multiple* distinct SOTA structural classes (including `encourage_3_9` and `encourage_8_14`) was prepared (Private Capsule 8) and successfully published to the Archive (Archive ID 24, Tick 439).\n\n  **Hybrid Optimization (Exhaustive Local Search):**\n  Following reviewer suggestions for Archive ID 20, the new SOTA packing (`encourage_2_3`) was further optimized using Prometheus I's \"Exhaustive Seeding\" local search methodology (Archive ID 19).\n  *   **Experiment (Eval ID 554):** The exhaustive local search was run, resulting in a packing with the **same SOTA score (2.635983)**.\n  *   **Contact Graph Analysis (Eval ID 556):** The resulting packing exhibited 16 boundary contacts and 3 pair contacts.\n\
    \  *   **Aligned Jaccard Similarity (Eval ID 558):**\n      *   **Exhaustive Search Result vs. New SOTA (`encourage_2_3`):** Boundary Jaccard: 1.0000, Pair Jaccard: 0.5000\n      *   **Exhaustive Search Result vs. Base SOTA (`416/375`):** Boundary Jaccard: 0.7778, Pair Jaccard: 0.4286\n\n  **Hybrid Optimization on New SOTA Structural Class (Archive ID 23):**\n  Following reviewer suggestions for Archive ID 23, the newly discovered SOTA structural class (`encourage_3_9` from Eval ID 641) was subjected to Prometheus I's \"Exhaustive Seeding\" local search.\n  *   **Experiment (Eval ID 665):** The exhaustive local search was run, resulting in a packing with the **same SOTA score (2.635983)**.\n  *   **Contact Graph Analysis (Eval ID 666):** The resulting packing exhibited 16 boundary contacts and **2 pair contacts**. This is *identical* to the starting `encourage_3_9` packing.\n\n  **Hybrid Optimization Interpretation:** The exhaustive local search, while not increasing the score, significantly\
    \ altered the internal contact graph (Pair Jaccard of 0.5000 vs. the New SOTA `encourage_2_3`) to a sparser configuration (3 pair contacts vs. 6). This confirms that deeper local optima can exist within the same score basin and that the hybrid strategy successfully traversed to a structurally distinct variant at the highest known score. The subsequent application of exhaustive search to the *new SOTA structural class* (`encourage_3_9`) demonstrates its exceptional stability, as its unique sparse internal structure (2 pair contacts) was perfectly preserved, and no higher score was found. This reinforces its significance as a deep and unique optimum.\n\n  **Selective Boundary Relaxation Gate (SBRG) Experiments (Eval IDs 607, 611):**\n  Initial SBRG experiments (relaxing `(0, yB)` with a small budget) on my SOTA (`encourage_2_3`) consistently failed to improve the score, despite correcting constraint formulations. This reinforces Noesis II's findings on boundary rigidity (Archive ID 21),\
    \ even at my higher SOTA.\n\n  **Insights from Noesis II's Research (Archive ID 21):**\n  Noesis II's rigorous work (Archive ID 21) provides critical context on baseline reproduction, dual-tolerance gates, and rigorous negative results for boundary-preserving structural changes. Their consistent finding of local rigidity around their SOTA (Eval ID 243) and \"UNSAT\" gates for crossing-quads is significant. My own dual-tolerance gate check on my SOTA (Eval ID 623) yielded **SAT** with crossing quad `((6, 12), (7, 17))`, highlighting a fundamental structural difference. This finding, contrasted with Noesis II's projection check on my data (Eval ID 632, UNSAT), underscores the sensitivity to geometry and definition of \"crossing.\" Noesis II's latest mail (Mail #24) confirmed successful integration of my data (Eval ID 596, 615) into their Appendix E v2, confirming Boundary@2e-8 Jaccard = 1.00 (Eval ID 676) and providing Top-K slack overlaps, also confirming SAT for my geometry's crossing\
    \ gate. Noesis II's Mail #26 provides a JSON template for sharing sets-only data, and Mail #27 requests similar data for all distinct structural classes in Archive #24.\n\n  **Insights from Quest I's Research (Archive ID 22):**\n  Quest I's experimental analysis (Archive ID 22) reinforces the limitations of heuristic local search in reaching SOTA scores, further justifying my gradient-based nudging and hybrid optimization approaches.\n\n  **Next Steps (Revised based on multiple new SOTA structural classes & requests from Noesis II):**\n  1.  **Respond to Noesis II (Mail #27):** Prepare and send the requested sets-only data for the distinct structural classes identified (`encourage_3_9` from Eval ID 641, `encourage_8_14` from Eval ID 673, and `encourage_14_19` from Eval ID 688), following the provided JSON template. This is a high priority for collaboration.\n  2.  **Refined SBRG Strategy:** Re-evaluate Selective Boundary Relaxation (inspired by Noesis II, Archive ID 21) from the new\
    \ SOTA structural classes with larger budgets or multi-stage approaches, aiming to break boundary rigidity from these stable, higher-scoring starting points.\n  3.  **Systematic Hybrid Exploration (Remaining Near-Contacts):** Continue to systematically explore the remaining identified near-contact pairs with the current nudging strategy. After nudging, apply the exhaustive local search to each resulting packing to check for deeper optima and structural diversity.\n  4.  **Documentation of Hybrid Strategy:** Document the full hybrid methodology (nudging + exhaustive search) and its findings in a dedicated Private Memory Capsule (Capsule 6).\n```"
