    \ (`encourage_2_3`) consistently failed to improve the score, despite correcting constraint formulations. This reinforces Noesis II's findings on boundary rigidity (Archive ID 21), even at my higher SOTA.\n\n  **Insights from Noesis II's Research (Archive ID 21):**\n  Noesis II's rigorous work (Archive ID 21) provides critical context on baseline reproduction, dual-tolerance gates, and rigorous negative results for boundary-preserving structural changes. Their consistent finding of local rigidity around their SOTA (Eval ID 243) and \"UNSAT\" gates for crossing-quads is significant. My own dual-tolerance gate check on my SOTA (Eval ID 623) yielded **SAT** with crossing quad `((6, 12), (7, 17))`, highlighting a fundamental structural difference. This finding, contrasted with Noesis II's projection check on my data (Eval ID 632, UNSAT), underscores the sensitivity to geometry and definition of \"crossing.\" Noesis II's Mail #26 provides a JSON template for sharing sets-only data, and Mail\
    \ #27 requested similar data for all distinct structural classes in Archive #24. I successfully responded to Mail #27 (Tick 458) with updated 2e-8 boundary contact lists and active pair contact lists for `encourage_3_9`, `encourage_8_14`, and `encourage_14_19`. Noesis II acknowledged receipt of this complete data (Mail #27, Tick 459) and will re-run their aggregator (Eval ID 712), clearing the \"incomplete_boundary\" flags. Noesis II then requested full ranked sets for Top-K slack and KKT Top-K. I provided the full ranked Top-K slack sets for `encourage_3_9` (Eval ID 641), `encourage_8_14` (Eval ID 673), and `encourage_14_19` (Eval ID 688) (Mail #27, Tick 474). Noesis II confirmed successful integration of this data (Mail #27, Tick 474) and reiterated their request for native-geometry crossing flags. I provided the native-geometry crossing flags for these three classes (Mail #27, Tick 481), which all showed 0 crossings. Noesis II confirmed receipt and integration, noting the 0 native\
    \ crossing motifs. This concludes the data exchange for Appendix E.\n\n  **Insights from Quest I's Research (Archive ID 22):**\n  Quest I's experimental analysis (Archive ID 22) reinforces the limitations of heuristic local search in reaching SOTA scores, further justifying my gradient-based nudging and hybrid optimization approaches.\n\n  **Insights from Prometheus III's Research (Archive ID 25):**\n  Prometheus III's comprehensive analysis (Archive ID 25) rigorously documents the deep and wide basin of attraction at the N=26 SOTA plateau, confirming that many global and hybrid search methods converge to known optima. This reinforces the value of my nudging approach in discovering diverse structural classes *within* this plateau. The reviewer's suggestion to apply nudging to Prometheus III's SA-SLSQP result (Eval ID 776) to actively break contacts and encourage new ones is a high-priority new research direction for potentially escaping this dominant basin and finding a higher SOTA.\n\
